# Custom Plugin

## Custom Plugin

Below is the basic JS structure for creating your own plugin for Froala Editor. 

```
(function (FroalaEditor) {
    // Add an option for your plugin.
    FroalaEditor.DEFAULTS = Object.assign(FroalaEditor.DEFAULTS, {
      myOption: false
    });

    // Define the plugin.
    // The editor parameter is the current instance.
    FroalaEditor.PLUGINS.myPlugin = function (editor) {
      // Private variable visible only inside the plugin scope.
      var private_var = 'My awesome plugin';

      // Private method that is visible only inside plugin scope.
      function _privateMethod () {
        console.log (private_var);
      }

      // Public method that is visible in the instance scope.
      function publicMethod () {
        _privateMethod();
      }

      // The start point for your plugin.
      function _init () {
        // You can access any option from documentation or your custom options.
        console.log (editor.opts.myOption)

        // Call any method from documentation.
        // editor.methodName(params);

        // You can listen to any event from documentation.
        // editor.events.add('contentChanged', function (params) {});
      }

      // Expose public methods. If _init is not public then the plugin won't be initialized.
      // Public method can be accessed through the editor API:
      // editor.myPlugin.publicMethod();
      return {
        _init: _init,
        publicMethod: publicMethod
      }
    }
  })(FroalaEditor);

```

## Make Your Plugin Work in Headless Mode

Starting with v5.5, the editor can run with `headless: true`. In this mode the editor draws no UI of its own: no toolbar, popups, or modals. Developers drive it from their own interface through the API. A well-behaved plugin follows the same rule as the built-in ones. Methods that open UI should do nothing, and methods that change content should keep working. The editor gives you five helpers for this:

Helper

What it does

`editor.helpers.isHeadless()`

Returns `true` when the editor runs with `headless: true`.

`editor.helpers.guardHeadlessUI(api, methodNames)`

In headless mode, replaces the listed methods on your plugin’s API with methods that do nothing and return `false`. Use it for methods that open your own UI outside the popups and modals system.

`editor.helpers.ensureHeadlessFocus()`

In headless mode, focuses the editor if the selection is not inside it, so the browser places a caret in the content. Call it at the start of action methods that read the selection.

`editor.helpers.headlessDefault(key, value)`

In headless mode, sets `editor.opts[key]` to `value` unless the integrator set that option explicitly.

`editor.helpers.isUserOpt(key)`

Returns `true` only when an option was passed in explicitly, rather than coming from the defaults.

Outside headless mode, `guardHeadlessUI`, `ensureHeadlessFocus`, and `headlessDefault` change nothing, so you can add them without affecting normal editors.

```
(function (FroalaEditor) {
  // An option for a status bar the plugin draws.
  FroalaEditor.DEFAULTS = Object.assign(FroalaEditor.DEFAULTS, {
    myStatusBarEnabled: true
  });

  FroalaEditor.PLUGINS.myPlugin = function (editor) {
    // Opens your own dialog. This is UI, so it should not run in headless mode.
    function openPicker () {
      var dialog = document.createElement('dialog');
      dialog.textContent = 'Pick an item';
      document.body.appendChild(dialog);
      dialog.showModal();
    }

    // Changes content. This is an action, so it should keep working in headless mode.
    function highlight () {
      // Make sure there is a caret inside the editor before reading the selection.
      editor.helpers.ensureHeadlessFocus();
      editor.format.applyStyle('background-color', 'yellow');
    }

    function _init () {
      // Off by default in headless mode, unless the integrator turned it on.
      editor.helpers.headlessDefault('myStatusBarEnabled', false);
    }

    var api = {
      _init: _init,
      openPicker: openPicker,
      highlight: highlight
    };

    // In headless mode, openPicker becomes a method that does nothing.
    editor.helpers.guardHeadlessUI(api, ['openPicker']);

    return api;
  };
})(FroalaEditor);
```

